2.1 Video Storage, CDN Caching, Tiered Resolutions & Anti-Poisoning P2P Mesh
ProofTube utilizes the open-source PeerTube core engine for encoding, delivery, and federation:
Adaptive Bitrate HLS & WebRTC P2P Mesh: Active viewers watching the same stream automatically share video segments client-side via WebRTC, drastically reducing server bandwidth costs during viral traffic spikes.
Client SHA-256 Segment Auditing & Proof-of-Viewer Receipts: To eliminate WebRTC chunk poisoning and fake peer-seeding sybil farms, the client player verifies the SHA-256 checksum of every received .ts video segment against the master playlist manifest before rendering. Before a node receives bandwidth credit toward its Active Seeding Reputation Score, the receiving client player generates a cryptographically signed micro-receipt confirming a real human session consumed the segment.
S3-Compatible Cloud Storage & CDN Edge Proxy: When viewer peer counts fall to zero (or on mobile browsers with restricted WebRTC data channels), streams fall back to cheap S3 storage (e.g., Cloudflare R2, Backblaze B2, or MinIO). S3 storage sits behind a global CDN edge cache proxy configured to support byte-range caching and CORS headers.
Tiered Resolution Peer Thresholds: To protect node bandwidth budgets on low-traffic, zero-peer long-tail videos, stream delivery is dynamically throttled based on active peer counts:
< 5 Active Peers (Solo Viewer Mode): Stream resolution is capped at 720p HD directly from CDN/S3 storage.
>= 5 Active Peers (Mesh Mode Unlocked): The player automatically unlocks 1080p and 4K Ultra HD resolutions, leveraging WebRTC peer seeding.
Cold Storage Auto-Pruning: If a creator’s account balance falls to zero and their media receives zero views for 90 consecutive days, the host node automatically demotes unseeded files to cheap cold storage or purges unseeded local copies.
2.2 Sovereign Identity & Mobile Service Worker Execution Architecture
ProofTube replaces centralized user databases and email logins with the Nostr protocol:
Channel Identity: Channels are represented by a Nostr public key (npub). Creators retain 100% cryptographic ownership of their identity, subscriber graph, and post metadata.
Federated Portability: If a node shuts down or censors a creator, the creator simply inputs their key into any other Nostr-compatible node, instantly restoring their entire channel, follower base, and comment history across the open Nostr network.
Mobile Background Service Worker Execution: To prevent iOS Safari thread-freezing during app-switching (e.g., jumping to a native wallet app for NWC/WebLN payments), L402 challenge tokens, payment preimages, and cryptographic key exchanges execute inside a persistent Background Web Worker / Service Worker thread.
